{{<shell}}{{$content}}
<h1>Legal</h1>
<p class="hint">Two documents, published from the first day so this community never has none. They
  say who a member is agreeing with &mdash; you, not the people who wrote the software and not
  whoever rents you the machine.</p>
<p class="problem">Written by people who are not lawyers, and they do not know what country you are
  in. Read them. If this community carries real risk, have somebody qualified read them too.</p>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>document</th><th>state</th><th>address</th><th class="actions"></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
  {{#docs}}
  <tr>
    <td><strong>{{title}}</strong><div class="dim">{{summary}}</div></td>
    <td>{{#overridden}}<span class="pill blue">yours</span>{{/overridden}}{{^overridden}}<span class="pill">as shipped</span>{{/overridden}}
      {{#overridden}}<div class="dim">{{updated}}</div>{{/overridden}}</td>
    <td class="mono"><a href="{{publicUrl}}">{{publicUrl}}</a></td>
    <td class="actions"><a href="{{editUrl}}">Edit</a></td>
  </tr>
  {{/docs}}
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>What they rely on</h2>
<ul class="cando">
  <li><span class="dim">linked</span> every email this community sends links to the terms</li>
  <li><span class="dim">open</span> both pages are readable without signing in, because most people
    following that link have no account yet</li>
  <li><span class="dim">true</span> the privacy policy describes what the software actually does. If
    you change what it promises, you are changing a requirement</li>
</ul>
<p class="hint">In the text, <code>{{lbrace}}community{{rbrace}}</code> becomes this community's name
  and <code>{{lbrace}}domain{{rbrace}}</code> its address, so renaming the community does not leave
  either document wrong.</p>
<p class="hint">Leaving a document as shipped is the useful default: it improves when the software
  does. Saving your own version stops that for that document.</p>
{{/content}}{{/shell}}
